Finally, he took the cup from my hand—his fingers brushing mine in a way that felt intentional.

I let go too quickly. Liam just leaned back, “good girl,” before taking a slow sip. His face fell.

“Problem?” I asked innocently. I may have asked for extra sugar.

He locked his eyes on mine, his face remained still. My stomach dropped. Liam didn’t speak. He lifted his drink, eyes unmoving from mine and tipped the cup over. He spilled the coffee all over me.

Not poured. Not dumped. Just tipped it ever so slightly. Enough that it splashed down my front—seeping through my white shirt, freezing ice liquid against my skin.

I gasped, jerking back, but it was too late. Gasps rippled from the nearby tables. Liam sighed.
“Shit,” he muttered, all faux innocence as he leaned forward, grabbing a few napkins like that would fix anything. “That was clumsy of me.”

My heart pounded. I was drenched. My white shirt clung to me, sheer with coffee and humiliation.

I could feel everything—the chill of the liquid still soaking into my skin, the way eyes flicked toward us, the way Ethan’s smirk was practically radiating amusement.

Mason exhaled heavily. “Jesus, Liam. You ok, Caroline?”

I was frozen in place, in shock of what happened. Liam ignored him. Instead, he pulled off his hoodie in one smooth motion, handing it to me. “Here.”I stared at it. “Put it on.” Not a suggestion. A command.
I took it. I stared at Liam as I reached down to take my shirt off. He wanted a show, I’d give him a show.

He grabbed my wrist, and lowered his voice, “Don’t.” A single warning, before releasing me.

I resigned, what else could I do? I slipped it over my head, the fabric swallowing me whole. It was still warm, It smelled like him — that familiar mixture of bergamot, amberwood and musk.

And the moment it was on, Liam’s easy smirk was back in place. “Much better,” he murmured, pleased.
Ethan grinned, watching the interaction. Mason shook his head, flicking a sympathetic look my way.

“Sit,” Liam said next. When I didn’t move fast enough, he sighed and grabbed my wrist. He pulled, Not hard, Not aggressive. Just enough to bring me to his lap.

“There we go,” Ethan mused, sipping his coffee. “Right where she belongs, what was it you wanted to show me, Liam?” his eyes flicked to Liam’s phone.

“Drop it.” Liam sounded annoyed. I wondered if it was only because of my misstep or because of Ethan’s remarked.

“Too bad,” Ethan said in my direction.

Liam’s hand was already resting on my thigh. His breath was warm against my ear. No one in this coffee shop would see anything wrong. Just a boy and his girlfriend, a girl leaning into her boyfriend’s touch. Trusting. Dependent. Just a mistake—a spill, an accident, an easy fix. Because what else could it be?

They wouldn’t see the battle in my head. They wouldn’t know that I was calculating every movement, every shift of my weight, every glance to determine what was safest.

I chuffed; Liam’s hoodie swallowed me whole, his arms encased me, and my body melted against his as if I belonged there.

Liam’s hand curled lightly around my waist, fingers brushing lazy patterns against my hip. It looked affectionate. But I new better, it was possession.
I sipped my coffee, trying to ignore the wet, cold fabric still clinging to my skin under his hoodie.

“You’ve gone quiet, princess,” Ethan mused, studying me from across the table, his smirk ever-present.

Liam’s fingers tightened, just slightly.
“She’s just shy,” he said, not even looking at me.

Ethan chuckled. “And here I thought you might have kept her on a limited word count.” My pulse stuttered.
I should have stayed quiet, should have let them move on, but my nerves were still raw. My pride was still raw. “I’m tired,” I muttered, eyes locked on the rim of Liam’s coffee cup.

“Not getting enough sleep these days?” Ethan yoked.

Mason shifted. The only one who didn’t seem entertained by my humiliation.

“She’s probably cold,” Mason said, voice even, his gaze flicking toward the coffee still soaking into my jeans.

It was kind, but the moment he said it, Liam’s grip on my waist hardened.

“She’s warm now,” Liam replied smoothly, pulling me closer against him. “What’s this? The second hoodie of mine you’re stealing? You still have the other one from last night.” My cheeks flushed.

“I can bring it back next time I see you…” my voice had gone soft.

Liam laughed, “I’m teasing you. Keep it. Wear it. You know I like when you wear my things.”

I swallowed. The bruise on my neck suddenly burned. I reached up to cover it when my hand but Liam gently pushed my hand aside.

Mason didn’t push but quietly added, “She probably didn’t plan on needing a second shower.”

Ethan, however, let out a laugh. “Damn, Mason. You’re getting soft.” “Just saying—”
“No need,” Liam cut him off. Final. Absolute.

Mason leaned back, shaking his head, but he let it go. Ethan didn’t.

“Speaking of soft,” Ethan mused, tilting his head as he appraised me. “Tell me, Caroline—are you adapting to the good life yet?”

My fingers tightened around my coffee cup.

“Leave her alone,” Liam said with casual command. “Relax, Barnette. Just making conversation.”
“Try another topic.”

Ethan tapped his fingers against the table. Then—
“Fine,” he drawled. “Let’s talk about tonight.” His gaze flicked to me. “What’s on the agenda, princess? You joining us for once, or is our golden boy here keeping you all to himself?”

My stomach twisted, I already knew where this was going. “She has plans,” Liam answered before I could speak.
Ethan’s brow lifted. “Oh?” He turned back to me, intrigued. “Do tell.” I hesitated, glancing at Liam, but his expression remained unreadable.
I shifted under the weight of Ethan’s stare. “It’s just a girls’ night.” I wonder if Chloe had mentioned something to him.

Ethan let out a low hum of amusement. “A girls’ night.”

I hated the way he said it, like the words themselves were a joke.

Mason finally looked up from his phone, giving Ethan a glance before shifting his focus to me. “That sounds fun. You going out?”

“No,” I said quickly. “Just staying in, watching movies, drinking wine, that kind of thing.” Ethan’s smirk grew. “Drinking, huh?”
Liam didn’t react, but I could feel the shift in his posture. Ethan saw it too. His grin sharpened.

“So, let me get this straight,” he continued, gesturing between Liam and me. “You won’t drink when we’re out, but you’ll get tipsy with your little roommates?”

I bristled, gripping my cup tighter. Liam was still silent. Still watching.

Ethan pressed on. “I mean, I get it,” he mused, voice teasing but pointed. “Girls’ nights are sacred, right? Drinking, gossiping, talking about us—” He tilted his head. “You do talk about us, don’t you?”

I swallowed. I could feel Liam waiting for my answer.
Mason, sensing the tension, let out a quiet sigh, “Ethan, knock it off.”

But Ethan wasn’t done. He smirked, eyes locked onto me. “What do you think they’ll say about you, Barnette?”

Liam finally exhaled, his fingers tapping once against the table. “Not a damn thing that matters.”

Ethan grinned. “You sure? You know how girls are. They drink a little wine, get a little loose- lipped—”

Liam’s hand tightened on my thigh. I forced myself to breathe evenly.

Ethan’s smirk didn’t waver. “Would be a shame if Caroline let something slip.” I swallowed. “I don’t—”
“Like what, how big my dick is?” his words not matching the warning in his voice. Ethan just laughed, finally leaning back. “Relax, man. I’m just fucking with you.”
But the damage was done. Liam didn’t look at me, but his hand remained firm on leg. I hated that Ethan was enjoying this. He tapped his fingers against the table, eyes never leaving me.

“So, Caroline,” Ethan murmured. “How did Liam like his breakfast? I always thought girls like you prefer to be the main course.”

I hated how much he got under my skin so easily, how he made me squirm, waiting for a reaction. But Liam shifted, his arm fully wrapping around me, his fingers idly playing with a strand of my hair.

“Fuck off, Ethan,” he said, voice low. Dangerous. Ethan grinned.

“Just checking in,” he said easily, raising his hands in mock surrender. “Wouldn’t want her feeling… neglected.”

I narrowed my eyes.

I knew what Ethan was doing, and so did Liam, his fingers stilled against my hair. His posture relaxed—too much, like a predator right before it strikes.

“Neglected isn’t a problem you need to worry about,” Liam murmured, his voice like velvet but with something sharp beneath it.

Finally satisfied, or maybe just bored, Ethan let it go. For now.

His smirk still lingered. My stomach churned as I lowered my eyes to the rim of my coffee cup, trying to pretend that my world wasn’t contorting. trying to pretend that the warmth of Liam’s arms wasn’t the only thing keeping me upright.

The moment Ethan let it go, the conversation shifted—easily, effortlessly—because that’s how this always worked.

Liam was still relaxed, one arm draped over my waist, his fingers now absentmindedly tracing patterns along my hip. A silent reminder. A silent warning.

Ethan leaned back, stretching out his arms, completely at ease, like he hadn’t just been playing with me, like he hadn’t just tested exactly how far Liam would let him push.

Mason exhaled, still scrolling through his phone. Uninterested, but aware. And me? I just focused on breathing.
Focused on sipping my coffee, ignoring the lingering sensation of the coffee seeping through my clothes, the way the air felt too thick, too charged, despite the casual turn of conversation.

They were so good at this, so good at pretending, so good at moving on like nothing had happened. I needed to be better at it. So I tried.

I forced my grip to loosen around my cup. I leaned slightly into Liam, feigning comfort. And then I watched. Because that was the safest thing to do.

They talked about the usual things—practice schedules, weekend plans, some girl Mason had been talking to. Ethan tossed out some crude comments. Mason rolled his eyes. I shot him a sympathetic look. Liam hummed in response to something I wasn’t really listening to.

Yet the weight of his arm around me never wavered, and the tension coiled in my stomach never fully eased.

The second I let myself breathe— the second I thought I could just fade into the background of their world—Liam’s hand shifted. He tilted his head slightly, glancing down at me with something unreadable. “Everything ok?”

I blinked. I didn’t have an answer; I didn’t know what he wanted me to say. “Just tired,” I said instead. A safe answer. A non-answer.

Ethan smirked, clearly amused. “Already? You barely did anything,” he said, too smug, too knowing.
I stiffened.

Liam didn’t react immediately.

But I felt it—the way his fingers flexed against my hip.

“She’s had a long night,” Liam said, smooth, easy. “Isn’t that right?” It was a loaded question.

I felt sick and could feel the hear flood my cheeks, but I nodded,“Yeah.”

Liam smiled, like I’d given the right answer. That should have scared me more than it did. Mason checked his phone, sighing, “We should get going. We have practice soon.”
Liam glanced at his watch. “You’re right.” Then his focus drifted back to me, “Come on.”

It wasn’t a question.

I hesitated, because what was I supposed to do? Follow him like a puppy to his hockey practice?
Because I should have gone back to my dorm, changed, gotten my books, done something to remind myself that my life was still mine.
But then Liam tapped my hip, signaling me to rise. After standing he held out his hand for me to take.

I could feel Ethan’s gaze on me. Mason, too. And I knew what this was: A test. A reminder. A choice that wasn’t really a choice at all.

So I took his hand, and Liam’s smirk was back in place. Of course I went with him. Where else could I go?

The air outside was crisp, carrying the faint scent of autumn, damp leaves and cooling pavement. The midday sun filtered through the thinning canopy of trees that lined the walkway leading to the rink.
Liam walked beside me, his pace easy, casual. Too casual. Like the scene in the coffee shop never happened. Like the coffee wasn’t still drying against my skin beneath his hoodie. Like I wasn’t still reeling.
Ethan and Mason followed behind us, conversing in low tones, the occasional chuckle from Ethan making my skin crawl.

I kept my head down, watching the cracks in the pavement, focusing on the solid weight of Liam’s palm in my hand. Possessive. Assuring. Undeniable.

When we reached the intersection where we normally split—me toward campus, them toward the rink—Liam slowed.

“You’re coming, right?” Ethan asked, flashing a grin my way.

I opened my mouth, then shut it. “Nah, she has got work to do,” Liam answered smoothly, like he already knew.

My stomach coiled at the ease in his voice.

“Shame,” Ethan mused, stuffing his hands into his pockets. “Was looking forward to seeing if she’d warm the bench for you, Barnette.”

Liam’s grip on me tensed for the briefest second before he chuckled, low and warning, “Maybe next time.”

Ethan grinned, but Mason nudged him forward toward the rink, steering him away before he could push his luck any further.

Liam turned to me then, and for the first time since the coffee shop, I didn’t see amusement in his eyes, only expectation, like he was waiting for something. He didn’t say anything, just lifted his hand slightly and brushed his lips against my knuckles.

A silent offer. A silent demand. I looked at our hands, then at him. His fingers curled around mine, squeezing lightly, like a promise, like a victory. Then he let go.

“You should go lay down,” he murmured, stepping back. “You look tired.” The way he said it
—it wasn’t concern. It was an order.

I didn’t respond, didn’t even nod, just stepped away, gently pulling my hand from his grip and pulled his hoodie tighter around me, and walked away.

The moment I turned the corner, out of Liam’s line of sight, my shoulders sagged. The tension in my body unwound, unraveling like a tightly coiled thread.

I should have returned back to my dorm.

That would have been the logical choice. The normal choice. Yet the thought of stepping inside, of Chloe’s excited questions, Alex’s scrutiny—it was too much.

I could still feel the weight of their eyes from earlier, the unspoken challenge in Alex’s voice.

“Are you actually dating Liam, or is this just whatever he wants it to be?”

My stomach turned. It didn’t matter what I told her. What did the truth even matter anymore?

The coffee was still clinging to my skin beneath Liam’s hoodie, a phantom reminder of the earlier humiliation. My mind felt hazy, fogged over by the weight of everything.

I pulled out my phone, my fingers hovering over Chloe’s contact. I typed out a message:
Studying is taking longer than I thought. Might be back late. Don’t wait up.

Then deleted it. No, I wanted to stay true to movie night. I wanted to go back to normal college life.

I didn’t know what I was doing. All I knew was that I couldn’t go back there yet. I needed to think. To breathe. To fix something—anything.
My thoughts were spiraling, too loud, too fast. I reached into my bag, fingers brushing the tin buried beneath my notebooks. I hesitated, only for a second. Then popped open the lid and tipped a pill onto my tongue.

Bitter. Familiar.

The rush came before I even fully swallowed, like my body knew what was coming and accepted it before my mind did.

A slow inhale. A steadier exhale. Calm. Control. Clarity.
It was fine.
I was fine.
I straightened my shoulders, my decision made.

The library where I could disappear, where I could drown in work, bury myself in equations, notes, and deadlines until nothing else mattered.

Until I wasn’t thinking about Liam. Until I wasn’t thinking at all.

I turned toward the brick walkway leading across campus, toward the looming, ivy-covered silhouette of the library- a place for people like me, people who needed an excuse to run.

A few papers written and a few chapters read and outlined later and I looked at my phone. 16 different notifications - a few texts from Chloe checking in, a text from Alex reminding me that I left dishes in our sink. To clarify, it was one dish— a coffee mug — but whatever, a few emails and then…

A picture of me hunched over my books, papers and notebooks neatly organized in piles, my hair pulled up in a messy bun. I was completely engrossed in whatever I was writing.

Liam: you’re the only person who makes obsessing over text books look cute. I think it’s the hoodie.

Caroline: it’s probably the text books but if you want to believe it’s your hoodie, I won’t stop you.

I pressed send without second guessing myself.

Liam: smart and agreeable? Where has this Caroline been hiding?

An unfamiliar warmth curled in my stomach. I drummed my fingers against my thigh trying to think of how to respond.

Caroline: don’t get used to it.

Liam: too late

I locked my phone and exhaled a breath I didn’t know I had been holding. He was starting to get to me. The thing is, I wasn’t sure if I wanted to stop him.

I walked out of the library with my pulse still rattling beneath my skin, Liam’s hoodie still swallowing me whole, the weight of his hand on my thigh still ghosting over me even though I was alone now.

I sucked in a sharp breath, adjusting the strap of my bag on my shoulder, forcing my feet to keep moving as I made my way back to the dorm.

I couldn’t stop thinking Ethan was waiting for me in the shadows. Liam should have worried me, his ever presence but Ethan…

I should’ve been used to him by now—the way he needled, the way he made everything feel like a setup, a test or a challenge.

I could still hear his voice in my head, teasing, poking at something just beneath the surface.

You do talk about us, don’t you?

I had felt Liam’s silence more than I heard it. The reminder that there were lines I couldn’t cross.

The air was crisp, the autumn wind pulling at my sleeves as I walked through campus. Students passed in clusters, their laughter and conversations humming like white noise in the background. I barely noticed them.

Focus on tonight.
That’s what I told myself. A normal night. A normal, stupid, meaningless girls’ night.

I needed that. I needed to sit on the floor of my dorm with Chloe and Alex, drinking cheap wine, eating takeout, watching some rom-com we’d seen a thousand times before.
I needed to feel like I still belonged there.

I needed a version of myself that wasn’t twisted up in Liam’s control.
By the time I reached my dorm, my hands were freezing, my head buzzing—whether from the caffeine or the remnants of the conversation at the café, I wasn’t sure.

I pushed the door open and stepped inside. The scent of Chloe’s jasmine perfume and the faint traces of Alex’s French hair products filled the space.

The normalcy of it made my chest tighten.

Chloe was already curled up on her bed in sweats and fuzzy socks, her laptop balanced on her knees, scrolling through something with her phone propped up beside her.

She looked up when I walked in.
“Hey!” she grinned. “I was about to text you. Alex and I were debating dinner—do we go with Thai or do we be lazy and order from the pasta place again?”

I forced a smile, setting my bag down by my desk. “I could go for Thai.”

“Solid choice.” Chloe stretched, then glanced at me more closely. Her smile faltered just slightly. “What’s wrong?” she asked, voice softer now. Too perceptive.

I hesitated. “Nothing. Just tired.”

Chloe hummed, unconvinced but not pushing. “Mmm. Well, wine will fix that.” She reached down and pulled out the bottle she had chilling in a small ice bucket.

I laughed, peeling off Liam’s hoodie and my stained shirt before tossing them into the hamper, moving toward my dresser to grab something comfortable.

One night. One night to breathe. One night without thinking about him.

Alex walked in a few minutes later, arms crossed over her chest, her sharp gaze flicking over me as she set her bag down.

“You look like shit,” she announced.

“Thanks,” I muttered, pulling out a pair of leggings and a soft, old sweatshirt. Chloe shot Alex a look. “Not exactly the vibe we’re going for, Lex.”
Alex shrugged, grabbing her phone and flopping onto her bed. “Just saying. I don’t know what the hell you do when you’re not here, but it’s clearly not getting enough sleep.”

My stomach twisted.

Chloe huffed. “She has a boyfriend, duh. They do things.”

Alex scoffed, eyes flicking up from her screen. “Right. That’s what it is.” Something about the way she said it sent my pulse into my throat.

I turned quickly toward my dresser, grabbing my clothes.

“I’m gonna shower real quick,” I said, voice too light. I didn’t wait for a response before slipping into the bathroom, shutting the door.

I braced my hands against the counter, exhaling slowly. Alex knew something. She didn’t know know. But she knew enough to sense something wasn’t right. And she was watching me. Looking for cracks.

I turned the water on, letting the steam fill the small space, and pressed my fingers against my temples.

One night.

I just had to get through one night. I pulled out my little altoid box from my pocket, fingering the familiar tin before popping an adderall. I needed to wake up, I couldn’t go through tonight as a zombie. By the time the shower was over and I was dressed in buttery leggings and a matching v neck top. I let my damp hair hang loose, allowing my golden brown hair to dry.

The wine bottle tipped precariously as Chloe poured another round into my glass, the deep red liquid sloshing slightly over the rim. She giggled, cheeks already flushed from the alcohol, and I couldn’t help but smile as I lifted the glass to my lips.

“Okay, but seriously,” Chloe slurred dramatically, tucking her legs beneath her as she sprawled across her bed, “why is Mamma Mia! the single greatest movie of our generation?”

Alex snorted from where she sat cross-legged on the floor, flipping absently through her phone. “It’s literally just ABBA propaganda.”

Chloe gasped in offense. “Excuse you.”

I let out a quiet laugh, sinking further into my spot on the couch. The twinkle lights strung along our dorm’s ceiling cast a warm, golden glow, making the space feel softer, safer. It was the kind of night I had missed—the kind where my guard could lower, where I wasn’t calculating every move, every word, every expression.

The TV screen lit up with the opening credits, the familiar hum of I Have A Dream swelling through the tiny dorm room.

Chloe clutched a throw pillow to her chest, swaying to the music. “Ugh, I love this movie.” Alex smirked. “We know.”
