Chapter 9: The Pearl.



The following Tuesday was hockey practice. The rink was quiet, the faint hum of the lights above casting a sterile glow over the ice. I stood at the boards, one gloved hand resting on the railing, my stick balanced casually against my hip. Ethan sat on the bench nearby, his posture loose and relaxed, though his sharp eyes didn’t miss a thing.

“She’s something, isn’t she?” Ethan said, breaking the silence.

My jaw tightened, but I didn’t look over at him. “What do you mean?”

Ethan chuckled, leaning forward and resting his elbows on his knees. “Caroline. You’ve got her wound up so tight, I’m surprised she hasn’t snapped yet. It’s… impressive.”

I shifted slightly, my fingers tightening around the railing. “She’s fine.”

“Sure she is,” Ethan said, his tone light, almost amused. “But you’ve got to admit, it’s fascinating. Watching her squirm. Watching her try so hard to keep it together, even though we both know she’s cracking.”

I finally turned his head, my expression hard; he didn’t understand, didn’t see her the way I did. “She’s not cracking.”

“If you say so,” Ethan agreed, his smirk widening. “But she will. And when she does? That’s when the real fun begins.”

My eyes narrowed. “What are you getting at?”

Ethan shrugged, sitting back and spreading his arms across the bench. “Just saying, she’s already halfway there. She’s scared, she’s vulnerable, she’s looking to you for everything. You’ve got her exactly where you want her. Now all you have to do is… nudge her in the right direction.”

I stayed quiet, returning my gaze back to the ice.

“I mean, come on,” Ethan continued, his voice taking on a coaxing edge. “You’ve got this perfect little setup. She’s already yours, whether she admits it or not. But you know what would make it even better?”

“What?” I asked, tone clipped.

“Gratitude,” Ethan said, his eyes glinting. “Once she realizes how much she needs you— really, truly needs you—she’ll do anything for you. She’ll be perfect.”

“She’s already perfect,” I replied coldly.

Ethan smirked, shaking his head. “Nah. Not yet, but soon. You’re good at this, Liam—better than anyone else I’ve seen. But you’ve got to lean into it. Let her fall apart. Let her hit rock bottom. And then? You pick up the pieces. Put her back together the way you want.”

My grip on the railing tightened, my knuckles turning white. “I don’t need her to fall apart.”

“Don’t you?” Ethan said, his voice softening, almost conspiratorial. “Think about it. Once she breaks, she’ll stop fighting. She’ll stop second-guessing. She’ll stop pretending she doesn’t belong to you. And she’ll be so grateful—so devoted—that she won’t even remember what it felt like to stand on her own.”

I chewed on his words, letting them take space in my head. Devotion….my jaw tightened.

“And hey,” Ethan added, his tone turning almost playful, “if you’re worried about her breaking too much, don’t be. She’s tougher than she looks. You’ll figure out the balance. You always do.”

I glanced at him, keeping my face relaxed, calm,“You think I don’t know what I’m doing?”

Ethan raised his hands in mock surrender. “Never said that. Just offering a little… perspective.”

My eyes narrowed slightly, but there was no anger in my gaze. If anything, there was a flicker of understanding between us.

“You’re really enjoying this, aren’t you?” I commented finally.

Ethan smirked. “Of course I am. It’s not every day you get to watch a masterpiece in progress.”

I nodded, turning back to the ice.

“Just remember,” Ethan said, his tone dropping slightly, “don’t let her make you soft. She’s yours to mold, Liam. But if you let her steer, even for a second, you’re going to regret it.”

“I won’t.”

Ethan stood, slinging his bag over his shoulder. “Good. Because when you’re done with her, she’s going to be something else. Something untouchable. And all of it will be because of

you.”

I didn’t look at him, but the corner of my mouth twitched slightly, the faintest hint of a smirk appearing.

Ethan turned to leave, his footsteps echoing down the empty hall. But before he disappeared, he glanced back over his shoulder, his eyes glinting with amusement. “You’ll let me know how it goes, won’t you?”

I didn’t answer, his grip on the railing tightening as I stared out at the rink, Ethan’s words lingering in the cold air.

The rink remained quiet when I left, but Ethan’s words weren’t.

They clung to me like the chill in the air, needling under my skin, wrapping around my ribs, whispering in the back of my mind.

“She’s wound up so tight, I’m surprised she hasn’t snapped yet.” “Let her break. Then put her back together the way you want.”
