“Sweetheart,” I murmured, shaking my head, “you know that’s not how this works.” Her jaw clenched. “I don’t owe you anything.”
I arched a brow. Then I reached for the necklace, rolling the pearl between my fingers, feeling the way she shivered.

“I took care of you today,” I murmured, my voice dipping lower. “Didn’t I?” She swallowed.
I ran my fingers up her arm, slow and deliberate, dragging my touch over the soft fabric of her sweater, up to her collarbone. Her pulse jumped.

“You don’t get to just—”

I tilted my head. “Just what?” She exhaled shakily.
I smiled.

“I’ve been so good to you today,” I murmured, coaxing. “And you are grateful, aren’t you?” Her lips parted, but she didn’t speak, because she knew what I wanted her to say.
I let the silence stretch, my thumb brushing over the pearl. Then, finally—She nodded.
I smiled.

“There’s my good girl.”

She stood frozen for a beat, still clinging to her resistance like it might shield her, like the door behind her hadn’t already clicked shut.

Her fingers twitched at her sides. I didn’t move.

The tension between us stretched thin and brittle—until she inhaled, shallow and shaky, then stepped forward. Just one step. Then another.

She didn’t meet my gaze.

Instead, she came to stand in front of me, close enough that I could feel the tremor in her breath. Her hands lifted—hesitating at first, then rising to rest lightly against my chest. And then, slowly, like she had to force herself to do it—

She kissed me.

It wasn’t eager. It wasn’t soft. It was tentative, reluctant. A gesture of surrender rather than affection.

Her lips trembled against mine, like she hadn’t yet decided if she meant it. Like she hated herself for doing it—but did it anyway.

I didn’t deepen the kiss. Not at first.

I let it linger. I let her be the one to close the distance. To give in. To press her mouth a little harder against mine. And when she did—when her breath hitched and her fingers curled into my shirt—I responded.

Slowly. Deliberately.

I wrapped an arm around her waist and guided her backward, step by step, until the backs of her knees hit the bed.

She paused. She looked up at me.

And I saw it—all of it: the fear, the ache, the exhaustion, the flicker of something she hadn’t decided whether to name trust or resignation.

I kissed her again, firmer this time. She let me. Her hands slid to my shoulders. The necklace shifted against her skin, the pearl catching faint light. My mark. My reminder.

I reached for the hem of her sweater. She didn’t stop me.

And when I bent to kiss the hollow of her throat, just beneath the pendant— She closed her eyes.
The rest unfolded in silence. No words. No resistance. Only her breath, quick and uneven. Only the sound of fabric giving way. Only the creak of the mattress beneath us.

And then—Nothing at all. Just darkness. Just stillness.
Just the quiet certainty that she wasn’t leaving.

Chapter 10: Saints and Sinners.




I needed space. Privacy. I wanted to stay out of Liam's reach for as long as possible. Maintain the autonomy he slowly stripped from me. It had been a couple of days since I'd seen him last. I still felt like hiding. I started walking before I realized where I was going, just following the brick laid pathway through the old oak and cedar trees that speckled campus with shade.

I clutched my phone in my hands. I shouldn’t have called, but I didn't expect her to pick up. She rarely picked up. I tried to think how many missed calls went unreturned with a vague excuse of, "the day got away from me." or "I would have picked up if it were important." Maybe it was wishful thinking that another call would go unanswered, but I called her anyway. Subconsciously, I think I was looking for a distraction.

The phone rang twice before my mother picked up.

"Caroline,” she answered, her voice clipped, like she was already impatient. I swallowed. “Hi, Mom.”
"Is something wrong?"

I blinked. “No, I just…” I exhaled, kicking a small pebble on the walkway. “I just wanted to check in.”

Silence.

"Check in?" she repeated, her tone laced with something almost amusing. "You never call just to check in."

I curled my fingers around my phone, my pulse already thrumming in my ears. I do, you just never pick up or answer my texts. "Yes, I do," I kept my voice as casual as I could.

"Not really. Not unless you need something. Do you already need more money on your food card -- you know that's to supplement your meals, not replace the dining plan with junk."

I clenched my jaw, continuing my mindless meandering.

I forced a breath through my nose. “No, I don’t need anything.”

"Okay," She sighed, like even this small exchange had already exhausted her. "How’s school?"

I hesitated.

For a second—just a second—I considered telling her the truth. But what was the truth?
That I was drowning?

That I was barely making it through the day?

That I had been so stupid—so reckless—and now I was paying the price for it? That I was thinking of transferring?
I swallowed, my throat tight. “It’s fine.”
"Good. That school is an opportunity, Caroline. You're lucky to be there -- do you know what I would have done to have the same opportunity have before you? You need to make the most of it. Don't squander it."

My fingers curled into a fist against my thigh. She said it like I wasn’t trying.
Like I wasn’t killing myself just to keep up. Like I wasn’t desperate to belong.
“I know,” I murmured. She didn't know that these other students were used to the rigorous demands -- Chloe had told me how at her boarding school, kids regularly had mental breakdowns, needed rehab for coke or Adderall addiction to keep up, or would drop out -- never to be heard from. We had been watching Dead Poets Society, and she started to make all the comparisons. Meanwhile, at my private (and certainly not elite school), the only thing scandalous were the cheating circles.

"How are your friends? Chloe was it?" my mom pulled me back to the present. I nodded, even though she couldn’t see me. “Yeah, Chloe’s great.”
"Mm, that's nice dear. She seems nice." A pause. "Well, while I have you—are you taking care of your clothes? You know your father and I can't afford to buy you a new wardrobe just because you’re careless."

I inhaled sharply. “Yes, Mom.”

"Because the last time I visited, your sweaters looked awful. Are you following the washing instructions or are you still throwing everything in together?" I pressed my lips together. I almost laughed thinking of the extravagant shopping trip earlier in the week. Somehow, I don’t think my mother wanted to hear about it.

"You have to read the labels, Caroline." She sighed, like she was already tired of the conversation. "You should start getting into the habit now. I won’t always be around to fix things for you."

My throat tightened. “I know.”

"Well, I hope so. " Another pause. Then, "Oh, your cousin mentioned a boy -- something about some she saw on 'the 'Gram'"

My stomach turned. "What's his name?"
I knew what was coming, "Liam, and it's Instagram -- no one calls it, 'the 'Gram.'"

"I’m disappointed I had to hear about him from someone else," she said lightly. "You didn’t have the decency to tell me yourself? Or is he already on to the next girl?"

I flinched. “Mom.”

"What?" she said, feigning innocence. "I just know how boys like that are. They lose interest so quickly. Especially with girls like you." My jaw clenched.

"You just have to be careful," she continued, her tone shifting into something softer—but not gentler. "I know you think you’re special to him, but honey, these boys don’t settle for girls like you. Not in the end." She sighed, "I'm not saying you're not great. Your peers are just in another world to which we do not belong."

My nails dug into my palm. “I have to go.” "Caroline, wait."
I should have hung up. I should have let it go. But something in her voice made me hesitate. Not softness. Not concern -- Something else. And for reasons I couldn’t explain, I let the phone stay pressed to my ear.

"You always do this," she sighed. "You always shut down when I’m just trying to help." I inhaled sharply, pressing my eyes shut.
"I’m on your side, Caroline. I just don’t want you to embarrass yourself." My chest tightened.

"You get too attached to people," she continued. "And then you get hurt. Just like that girl in high school—the one you swore was your best friend? And what happened?"

I swallowed hard.

"She moved on without you."

I exhaled, staring at the chapel, my vision blurring.

"You have to be realistic, honey," she murmured. "Not everyone sees the world the way you do."

Silence stretched between us. She was waiting for me to say something. Waiting for me to agree with her.

I forced my voice to stay steady. “I have to go.” "Caroline—"I hung up.
I looked up and noticed I was standing outside the campus chapel. The chapel sat at the highest point on campus, its stone spire piercing the sky like a quiet sentinel over the university. Built in the late 1800s, it was a masterpiece of Gothic Revival architecture— stained glass windows casting jewel-toned light onto the worn pews, wooden beams arching high above like the ribs of a great ship. The scent of incense and candle wax lingered in the air, remnants of the quiet devotion that had seeped into its walls over generations.

Unlike the rest of campus, which hummed with wealth and ambition, the chapel remained untouched by prestige—it was a refuge, a place for whispered confessions and stolen moments of solitude. The heavy oak doors were never locked, offering sanctuary to those who needed it, whether for prayer, reflection, or simply a moment to breathe.

Maybe this is why it became my private escape. No one came to find me here, I could be alone in my thoughts, alone with my guilt. No one made demands of me here, I didn’t feel the need to pull out textbooks or felt the need to be productive. I could just melt into the silence and stillness.

The chapel was quiet, the kind of silence that wasn’t just the absence of sound but something more, something heavier. Something that settled in my chest, pressing against my ribs, I allowed it to swallow me whole. I squeezed my eyes shut, pressing the heels of my hands against my forehead, trying to keep my breathing steady.

The phone call was still haunting me. I reminded myself, she was wrong. She didn’t know what she was talking about. She was wrong.

But was she? Because wasn’t that what Liam had said—what Alex had implied—what I had been thinking this entire time?

That I was nothing more than something to be used? As if I were some hallow vessel - offered, discarded. A body made for sacrifice, not salvation.

The pearl at my throat felt heavier now.

I slid into one of the pew and exhaled shakily, letting my head fall back against the pew, staring at the ceiling, willing my thoughts to quiet. I didn’t bother fighting the tears. I was alone, finally, and let the weight of reality wash over me.

Liam Classes Ethan Alex
Odd girl out

I really should look into transferring. Maybe I could get financial aid — if I’m lucky, maybe a scholarship.

But I knew my parents wouldn’t allow it. They wouldn’t sign any loans on my behalf, they wouldn’t pay any college fees when I had a free ride at Holbrook University. They’d tell me to suck it up, toughen up. I just needed to work harder, develop some grit. Pull myself up by my boot straps.

That’s when I cracked. The first sob hit before I could stop it, tearing through my chest like something violent, something raw. I clamped a hand over my mouth, but it was useless— another followed, then another, each one escaping in sharp, gasping breaths, my whole body shaking with the effort of trying to contain it. My lungs burned, desperate for air, but the sobs wouldn’t stop, wouldn’t let me breathe, wouldn’t let me think. It wasn’t quiet, wasn’t dignified. It was ugly, messy, the kind of crying that scraped against my ribs and left me hollow. My shoulders curled in on themselves, my hands fisting into the fabric of my sweater like I could hold myself together if I just gripped hard enough. But I was coming apart, and no amount of swallowing it down, no amount of get it together was enough to stop the way my body convulsed with the weight of it all.

I heard the squeak of the door to the chapel opened, the familiar draft that drifted into the sanctuary. My breath caught in my throat and I forced myself to stillness, back to silence.

And I knew, without looking, without turning.

I rummaged for a spare tissue in my bag to clean myself up before he saw me. Before he could have the pleasure of seeing me tearful and pitiful after a pathetic conversation with the one person who is supposed to love me unconditionally.

Liam.

His footsteps approached, slow but steady, stopping at the end of the pew. He didn’t sit. Not yet.

"You’re crying."

I inhaled sharply, my hands still curled in my lap. "Go away, Liam." He ignored my words, “Are you religious?”
I exhaled, shaking my head. “Go away.”

Again, as if I hadn't just spoken, “Do you pray?”

I turned toward him then, brows furrowed, my eyes glassy. “What do you want?” Liam tilted his head slightly, watching me. “I want to know why you’re crying.”
I scoffed, wiping at my nose with the back of my sleeve. “Why, hoping you're the reason?” He didn’t answer. Instead, he slid into the pew beside me, moving carefully, like I might bolt. I stiffened immediately. “I told you to go away.”
He exhaled through his nose, relaxed, completely unfazed by the hostility in my voice. “And you’ve ignored me plenty of times before we got here, sweetheart.”

I glared before turning from him, my arms crossed across my chest, “I’m serious, Liam. You don’t get to be here.”

He leaned forward slightly, resting his forearms on his thighs. “Why not? Worried I'm going to burst into flames?” he made a show of examining his arms, glancing down his own body, "nope, no burning and I don't smell any sulfur -- do you?"

"You're such an asshole," I whipped toward him, my breath sharp, “You're so fucking entitled
-- you take everything from me. You get to decide what I do, where I go, how I spend my time. You already own everything else—” My voice broke slightly, but I pushed through it. “You don’t get this too.”

Liam didn’t react immediately.

He just stared at me. Considering. Calculating.

Slowly, he leaned back against the pew, stretching his arms across the backrest like he was settling in, “You know you shouldn’t curse in the house of God. But okay.”

I blinked. “Okay what?”

His eyes were steady as they met min, “You heard me. You want this to be your space? Fine. I won't touch you, I won't -- how did you put it? -- decide for you or take from you here.” He tilted his head slightly, watching me. “But you’re not getting rid of me that easily.”

My throat bobbed. “That’s not—”

“Go ahead,” he interrupted, his voice smooth. “Sit here in your silence and pretend you’re alone, but I can outlast you, sweetheart. We both know that.”

I clenched my jaw, turning back toward the altar. He would.
I knew he would.

The longer we sat there, the heavier the silence became. He was right, I was never alone. Not in my dorm, not in class, not at the library, and now, not even here.

I exhaled shakily, my voice quieter when I finally spoke, “I come here to think sometimes.” Liam didn’t react right away. He just stayed still, listening. “What are you thinking about?” I chewed my cheek, “I don’t know anymore.” For once, that wasn’t a lie.
I felt him shift beside me and for the first time, the power between us felt… even. Liam didn’t respond right away. He didn’t push. Didn’t fill the silence with some smug remark. He just sat there. Waiting. For the first time, I didn’t feel like he was winning. For the first time, I wasn’t sure what game we were even playing.

I let the silence stretch between us, staring at the stained glass window above the altar. The candlelight flickered, casting distorted shadows across the wooden pews, the flickering reds and golds warping into something almost sinister.

I had spent months mapping my life out in perfect, precise steps—school, classes, volunteer opportunities, extracurriculars. I had been so sure of who I was supposed to be, of what I needed to do to prove I deserved to be here.

But now?

Now everything felt like sand slipping through my fingers. I chewed the inside of my cheek, forcing the words out before I lost my nerve.

“Do you ever feel like you’re playing a role?” Liam’s gaze flickered. “What do you mean?”
I swallowed. “Like people think you’re something that you’re not?”

Liam tilted his head slightly, studying me. I hated how intently he listened, like he was trying to carve my words into something permanent. “Is that how you feel?” he asked.

I exhaled sharply, shaking my head. “Forget it.”

But he didn’t let it go. “Is that what this is about?” His voice was softer now, careful. “You think you’re a fraud?”

I scoffed. “I think I fooled everyone, that I’m not as smart as I pretend to be, that I’m not as clever, that I’m not who you think I am and the moment you find out, I’m going to be in even more trouble, that I’m going to be kicked out once the Dean finds out what a mistake they

made with bringing me here.” I pulled my knees up to my chest and wrapped my arms around myself. I didn’t look at him, I didn’t want to see his expression. I just rested my chin on my knees and held myself tightly.

His jaw tightened. “Who told you that?”

I let out a bitter laugh, looking away. “No one had to tell me.” Silence.
I could feel him watching me, feel the weight of his stare, feel the way he was already picking apart everything I wasn’t saying. “That’s bullshit.”

I turned to him sharply, my pulse spiking. “Excuse me?”

Liam’s gaze was steady, unreadable. “That’s imposter syndrome and it’s bullshit.” I shook my head, my throat tight. “You don’t get it.”
His voice stayed level. “Then explain it to me.”

I sighed, turning my head to finally look at him. I wanted him to see me, to really see me, not this fucked up fantasy he had of me.

To see the way I was drowning.

To see the way I was trying so goddamn hard just to keep up.

The way it didn’t matter how smart I was, how much I studied, how carefully I spoke— because I would never be one of them.

I would never be enough.

But Liam didn’t blink, didn’t flinch, didn’t doubt me. And that, more than anything, made my stomach twist.

I exhaled, my voice quieter now. “You don’t know what it’s like.”

Liam watched me for a moment, the candlelight flickering across his face, casting half of it in shadow. His expression didn’t shift—not pity, not understanding. Something else. Something heavier.

“You think you’ve fooled me?” he asked, voice low. I inhaled sharply. “Liam—”
“No, really,” he continued, leaning forward, elbows resting on his knees. “You think I don’t see you? That you’re some kind of mistake?” His lips curled slightly, but there was no amusement in it. “You’re wrong, sweetheart. I don’t waste my time on illusions.”

My stomach twisted. “You don’t—”

“I see you, likely more than you realize.” His voice was soft but absolute. “I see the way you tear yourself apart trying to be perfect. The way you work yourself until you’re shaking, until you can’t think straight. The way you make yourself small around people like Alex and Chloe—like you’re waiting for permission to exist.”

I stiffened.

“You think you don’t belong?” He shook his head, almost disappointed. “I’ve watched you, Caroline. You don’t just keep up with them. You make them want to keep up with you.”

I exhaled sharply. “That’s not—”

“That’s exactly what’s happening. You just refuse to see it.” I looked away, my throat tight.
“But me?” His voice dropped lower, more certain. “I see everything.” I swallowed hard. No, you don’t.
“And do you want to know what I see the most?” His fingers brushed against my chin, tilting my face back toward him. “Fear.”

I sucked in a breath.

“You’re terrified that you’re not enough. That you’re going to lose everything the second you stop running.” His eyes searched mine, unrelenting. “But you won’t. I won’t let you.”

I blinked. That wasn’t— That wasn’t what I wanted to hear. That wasn’t what this conversation was supposed to be.

“I won’t let you fall, Caroline.” His thumb brushed my jaw, the touch deliberate. “Because you’re mine.” I swallowed hard, pulse pounding in my ears, but I didn't turn away.

“And I don’t take mistakes.”

The words sent a shiver down my spine, because there it was. Not reassurance. Not comfort. Possession.

I watched as the stained glass turned him into something holy, but the red at his throat looked too much like blood. Blood always ruins the illusion. No matter how golden the idol, it stains the same.

I clenched my fists. I wanted to argue, to push him away, to tell him he was wrong. But another burning question escaped my lips first.

“Why me?” Liam was quiet.

I turned to look at him, searching his face. Looking for the lie. “You could have anyone,” I said, my voice unsteady. “So why me?”
He didn’t answer right away. Didn’t smirk, didn’t scoff, didn’t look amused like I thought he would. Instead, he just watched me. His expression was unreadable, but I could feel it—the weight of his attention, the way he was seeing right through me. Then, slowly, he leaned in.

“You really don’t see it, do you?”

I exhaled sharply, shaking my head. “Liam—”

“No.” His voice was firmer now, cutting me off. Not angry. Not cruel. Just… final. His fingers found my chin, tilting my face toward him.

“I could have anyone,” he admitted. “Girls who wouldn’t fight me. Who wouldn’t question me. Who would let me take without hesitation.”

I swallowed hard. Exactly. So why me?

His grip tightened—just slightly. Enough to make me hold my breath. “But none of them are you.”

My pulse stuttered.

“None of them,” he continued, “are as sharp as you. As stubborn as you. As difficult as you. As beautiful as you.” His thumb brushed over my jaw, slow, thoughtful. Like he was considering something. “None of them have your mind, your wit, your fire.” A muscle in his jaw twitched. “None of them would be worth the effort.” His gaze darkened. Intent. Sure.
Unshakable. “None of them would have undone me the way you do, but you?” I forced myself to stay still. To breathe evenly.
“You are.” His voice wasn’t soft—it was reverent; not kind, not loving, but final, like a vow I hadn't made.

I clenched my hands in my lap. “That doesn’t answer my question.”

His smirk returned—softer now, quieter. More dangerous. “Oh, sweetheart.”

His fingers trailed from my chin to my throat, resting there lightly. Not squeezing. Not yet. Just… there. A reminder. “You were mine before you ever knew it.”

My stomach turned. No, I wasn’t.

“I saw you before you ever saw me - a prophecy already written.” I swallowed, but his fingers didn’t move.

“And do you know what I thought?” he murmured, watching me like I was something fragile. Something precious. Something breakable. “Finally.”

A shiver ran through me.

“Finally,” he repeated, “someone worth keeping.” I didn’t realize I was trembling until he ran his hand down my arm, slow, soothing.

“Someone I can shape into exactly what I need.” My stomach twisted. There it was. “Someone I deserve.”

“That’s why you, Caroline.” His fingers curled slightly against my throat, not tight, just enough to feel it. To remind me how easily he could hold me in place. “Because you’re already perfect, not because you're finished but because you were made to be shaped.”

His thumb brushed over my pulse, slow, deliberate. “And soon, you’ll see it too.” I swallowed hard, pulse pounding in my ears. No. No, this wasn’t—
“You ask why you?” His voice was barely above a whisper now, intimate, dangerous. “The real question is why not?”

The worst part, the way he said it—the certainty in his voice, the finality in his touch—made me wonder if he was right and if I had ever really belonged to myself at all.

I should have stayed quiet. I should have let it end there, let him have the last word, let him think he’d won—because hadn’t he always? But something inside me—some final, desperate part of myself that still belonged to me—refused to let this be the end of it.

I swallowed hard, my voice quieter now. “And what if I’m not who you think I am?”

Liam’s fingers stilled against my throat. The air between us changed. Slowly, he blinked. Once. Twice. And then, just as slowly, he smiled. I had seen many of Liam’s smiles. The effortless ones, the charming ones, the ones he wore in public like perfectly tailored armor. This wasn’t one of them.

“Sweetheart,” he murmured, his voice all honey and velvet and something much, much darker beneath it, “that's not for you to decide.”

I inhaled sharply, my pulse jumping beneath his fingertips. His thumb traced the line of my jaw, a slow, idle movement—casual, almost affectionate.

“You think I haven’t considered that?” he mused. I didn’t answer. “That maybe you’re not as good as I believe you are?” He hummed, tilting his head slightly, eyes flickering over my face like he was searching for something. “That maybe you’re not strong enough. Smart enough. Worthy enough?”

I felt sick, the knot in my stomach tightening.

“I know you better than you know yourself, Caroline.”

I exhaled shakily, forcing my voice to stay steady. “You think you do.”

His fingers curled slightly, just enough for me to feel the pressure of his hand against my throat. “No,” he murmured. “I know.”

Another shiver ran through me.

“And if you weren’t who I thought you were?” His grip tightened, just slightly. Just enough to remind me he was still holding me there. “Then I would carve you into her.”

The words sank into me like ice. He spoke like I was a gift, like I was meant for this, but sacrifice without salvation isn't holy. It's just loss.

I shook my head, my breath uneven. “You can’t just—”

“I can.” The certainty in his voice knocked the air from my lungs. “I told you,” he continued, tilting his head slightly, studying me like I was a puzzle he was already solving. “You’re already perfect.”

He leaned in, lips brushing the shell of my ear. “And soon, you’ll be exactly what I need.” I clenched my hands into fists. No. No, that’s not—
“You don’t believe me now,” he murmured, his thumb pressing lightly against my pulse. “But you will.”

I swallowed hard.

“Once your version of reality no longer holds and the only voice in your head is mine.”

I wanted to fight him. To push him away. To tell him he was wrong, but my body—traitorous, frozen, trained—stayed perfectly still. Because he wasn’t just saying this. He believed it. And the worst part?

Somewhere deep inside, I was afraid he might be right. I should have shut my mouth. I should have let it end there, let him think he had the final word, let him win—because hadn’t he always? But something inside me snapped. Maybe it was the weight of the past few months, the exhaustion of pretending, the unbearable certainty in his voice. Maybe it was fear. Or maybe it was the last piece of myself I still had left to lose.

I clenched my jaw, my hands curling into fists at my sides. I willed my voice to stay steady, to be something solid, something real, something that he couldn’t twist into his own creation.

“I’ll never love you.” The words fell between us like a gunshot.

Liam stilled. For a second—just a second—I thought I saw something flicker in his expression. Something sharp, something cold, something close to real anger. Then it was gone. He exhaled, slow and measured, as if he were simply considering my words. As if they hadn’t meant a goddamn thing. And then, he smiled. Not amused. Not cruel. Patient.

“Sweetheart.” He exhaled through his nose, shaking his head slightly, “do you think I’m asking you for love?”

A chill ran down my spine. “That’s adorable.”
I clenched my jaw. “You're fucked up.”

“Am I?” He hummed, tilting his head slightly, his eyes drinking me in. “Or are you just angry that you don’t understand me?”

I exhaled sharply. “You don’t make sense, Liam. You don’t get to force me into this and expect me to—”

