“It is.”

I clenched my hands in my lap, curling my nails into my palm. “You think if you keep me long enough, control me long enough, I’ll just wake up one day and feel something for you?”

Liam’s expression didn’t shift because I had it all wrong.

“No,” he murmured, shaking his head slightly. “I think one day, you’ll wake up and realize you already do.” I stiffened. “That’s how this plays out, Caroline.”

He reached for my wrist again, his grip loose, gentle—like he was trying to soothe me.

“You’ll fight. You’ll hate me. You’ll tell yourself it’s impossible.” His thumb brushed over my pulse, slow, deliberate. “And then?” He leaned in slightly, his voice dropping to something barely above a whisper. “You’ll break.”

I yanked my hand back, but he didn’t let go. “No—”
“Yes.”

My breathing came faster now, my pulse hammering against his fingers. No. No, I wouldn’t.

“Because I don’t need you to love me today, sweetheart.” His grip tightened, not rough, just unshakable. “I just need you to love me in the end.”

I inhaled sharply, shaking my head. “No. No, I mean it.” My fits clenched at my sides. He hummed, tilting his head slightly, as if indulging me. “Do you?”

“Yes.” My voice came out rough, uneven, but I didn’t look away. I couldn’t. “I will never love you, Liam.”

His smirk deepened. Like I had just proven something for him. “Never is a long time, Caroline.”

I shook my head again, forcing out the words before I lost my nerve. “You can unmake me, break me down until I don’t recognize myself but you can’t make me love you.”

Liam’s gaze darkened, but not with anger. With certainty. “I don’t need to make you do anything,” he murmured, his thumb brushing idly against my jaw. Soft. Thoughtful.
Unbothered. “You’ll love me on your own.”

I scoffed, my nails digging into my palms. “You’re insane.”

“No.” His fingers tightened, just slightly. “I’m patient.” My eyes met his, “You think you’ll never love me?” he murmured, his voice all warmth, all silk, all inevitability. “That’s fine. I can wait.”

I exhaled sharply. No. No, this wasn’t—

“You’ll love me when there’s no one left to run to.” My breath caught.
“You’ll love me when you stop fighting the inevitable.”

I clenched my teeth, but he was already shaking his head, already seeing through me.

