Chloe ducked, giggling. “Hey! I’m serious. Movie night deserves a big screen.” I smiled, pulling my phone from my lap when it buzzed.
Liam: Leaving now. What snacks am I getting? I grinned, already knowing Chloe’s priority.
Caroline: Chloe says Sour Patch Kids are non-negotiable. Another buzz came a second later.
Liam: Obviously. Plus extra pretzel M&Ms for you.

I smiled dumbly at the screen as heart did that familiar flutter, the one that caught me off guard sometimes.

“Is that him?” Chloe asked, trying to peek at my phone. “Yeah,” I said, smiling. “He’s on his way.”
“Good.” Chloe rolled onto her stomach, propping her head up on her elbows. “I’m seriously starting to love him.”

I raised an eyebrow. “Should I be worried?”

She grinned. “Maybe. He’s bringing my favorite candy, after all.” It was fully dark outside when there was a knock at the door.

“Someone get that!” Chloe called, too busy arranging pillows into some elaborate nest to bother moving.

Alex groaned but got up from the floor. She opened the door to reveal Liam, holding two paper bags filled with snacks, his sweater sleeves pushed up, hair slightly messy in that effortless way that always worked for him.

“Delivery,” he said, stepping inside. Alex shot him a deadpan look. “Finally.”
He grinned. “Hey, I was given the ever important task of acquiring snacks. These things can’t be rushed.”

Chloe perked up from her spot on the floor. “Sour Patch Kids?”

Liam reached into one of the bags and tossed her the bright red package. She gasped, hugging it to her chest. “You’re my favorite person.”
I mock gasped, putting a hand to my heart. “Wow. I’m standing right here.”

Chloe waved me off, laughing. “You’re second. Bring me sour patch kids and we’ll talk.”

I shook my head, still smiling as Liam crossed the room to me, digging into the bag again before pulling out a familiar blue package.

“Pretzel M&Ms,” he said, handing them over. I grinned. “My favorite!”
He gave me a knowing look, voice low. “Obviously.”

The flutter in my chest deepened as I took the bag, fingers brushing his.

Before I could say anything else, Liam leaned in, threading a hand gently into my hair, and pressed a chaste kiss to my mouth—soft but deliberate.

Everything else faded for a second—the low hum of the projector, Chloe still sorting through snacks, Alex fiddling with the Bluetooth speakers.

When he pulled back, his thumb grazed my cheek. “Hey.” I smiled, breath catching slightly. “Hi.”
He lingered there for a moment before finally letting go, he went back unloading, pulling out an assortment of junk food from Doritos to cupcakes before bring out two bottles of wine. He set a smaller bottle in front of me: sparkling water. “I can’t trust you with alcohol.” He said casually, but it hit like a warning.

“Honestly, fair,” Alex concurred. I couldn’t help but agree.

Liam glanced around at the setup. “This is… a lot,” he teased, eyeing the chaotic sprawl of pillows, blankets, and snacks.

“We take movie night seriously,” Chloe chimed in from the floor, tearing into her Sour Patch Kids.

Alex was still fussing with the projector, frowning at the flickering image on the wall. “This thing better work. I am not watching anything on a laptop screen.”

“Just connect it to the Bluetooth speaker,” Liam suggested, nodding to the sleek black speaker Alex had set up.

She shot him a glare. “You think I’m not trying that?”

Chloe giggled, tossing a stray pillow at Liam. “Careful, Alex bites when tech doesn’t work.”

Liam dodged the pillow easily and dropped down next to me on the floor, his knee brushing against mine. His hand found my thigh under the blanket, fingers resting there, warm and steady.

“Pretzel M&Ms and a bad movie,” he murmured near my ear. “You’re getting spoiled two night in a row.”

I smiled, leaning into him. “Not complaining.”

The projector finally flickered to life, casting a slightly crooked image onto the wall. Alex raised her hands in triumph. “We have a picture.”
“Perfect,” Chloe said, climbing into her self-declared blanket throne. “Now we just need to pick the worst movie imaginable.”

The projector finally settled on the blank wall, the sound now blasting through Alex’s perfectly synced Bluetooth speakers.

“Okay,” Alex declared, dropping the remote onto the pile of blankets. “It’s go-time.”

Chloe sat cross-legged in her pillow throne, her arms already wrapped around the bag of Sour Patch Kids. “So, what terrible movie are we watching tonight?”

Alex scrolled through the movie queue. “We’re going for ‘so-bad-it’s-good’ or ‘actually decent but still kinda terrible’?”

“Somewhere in between,” Chloe replied. “I want it to be awful but also iconic.”

Liam leaned back next to me, one arm casually draped over my shoulders. “I’ve got the perfect pick.”

Alex gave him a skeptical look. “If you say Twilight, I’m leaving.” Liam laughed. “Relax. We’re going with a classic.”
He snatched the remote from Alex and started scrolling. The screen flicked through movie covers until he stopped on one, his grin widening.

300.

Chloe gasped dramatically. “Oh my God. Yes.”

Alex groaned, already shaking her head. “You’ve got to be kidding me. Two hours of sweaty men yelling at each other?”

Liam leaned forward, eyes gleaming. “Not just yelling—yelling with purpose.” He grinned and boomed, “This! Is! SPARTA!”

The room erupted into laughter.

Chloe clapped her hands. “Oh, we’re doing this. I’m here for it.” She grabbed a handful of popcorn and stuffed it into her mouth. “Also, so many abs. Peak cinema.”

I couldn’t stop laughing but still found myself saying, “You know they wore armor, right? Like, in actual history?”

Chloe waved me off. “Don’t ruin this for me, Caroline.”

I smiled, but my brain was already clicking through random historical facts. “I mean, the real Spartans probably weren’t this shredded. And they definitely didn’t fight in leather speedos.”

Liam chuckled, squeezing my knee under the blanket. “Let her have this moment.” Alex groaned, already pulling a pillow over her face. “I’m gonna hate this so much.” “Good,” Liam said, smirking. “Makes it more fun.”

The opening credits rolled, Gerard Butler’s intense narration booming through the speakers.

As the first battle scene exploded onto the screen, Chloe sighed happily. “This is better than I remembered.”

Alex snorted. “It’s literally just slow-motion stabbing and oil-covered pecs.” “Exactly,” Chloe shot back. “Art.”
Liam, fully leaning into the ridiculousness, grabbed a throw pillow and shouted again, “This! Is! SPARTA!”

Chloe burst out laughing, nearly choking on her Sour Patch Kids.

I shook my head, giggling. “Okay, but at least they showed an accurate phalanx formation.” Alex groaned. “Caroline, no one cares about battle tactics right now.”
“Sorry!” I said, laughing, but I couldn’t help adding, “but I would be amiss to point out that the real Leonidas probably didn’t have a Scottish accent.”

Liam leaned over, brushing his lips against my cheek. “Nerd.” I smiled, leaning into him. “You picked the movie.”
He grinned, eyes still on the screen, completely into the chaos unfolding.

The room was loud—popcorn crunching, Chloe openly swooning over the shirtless Spartans, Alex roasting every dramatic slow-motion shot, and Liam yelling along with the most iconic lines.

And me?

I sat there, caught between all of it, laughing harder than I had in a long time. It was warm. Easy. Fun.
Even if Gerard Butler was currently screaming about honor while launching another Persian off a cliff.

The projector hummed softly, casting the high-contrast battle scenes of 300 onto the wall.

I sank deeper into the pillows and blankets, my legs tucked beneath me. Liam sat next to me, one arm draped lazily over my shoulders, his fingers idly tracing small circles on my upper arm. Every so often, his hand would slide down to squeeze my knee—a steady, grounding touch beneath the blanket.

Chloe was sprawled on the floor in front of us, buried in a fortress of pillows, completely immersed in the movie. “Oh my God,” she gasped, pointing at the screen as another overly

muscular Spartan swung his sword in slow motion. “That’s not CGI, right? Like, they actually trained for this?”

Alex, perched on her bed with a bowl of popcorn, snorted. “They trained, dehydrated, and probably ate nothing but chicken for months. You know Hollywood’s secret formula: no shirts, baby oil, and zero historical accuracy.”

Liam chuckled. “Don’t ruin the magic, Alex.”

Chloe tossed a popcorn kernel in the air and caught it in her mouth. “Magic? It’s all just pecs, swords, and intense stares.” She sighed dramatically. “I love it.”

I smiled, but couldn’t help myself. “Hey now! There was some historical accuracy —but.” Chloe groaned, tossing a pillow at me. “Caroline! I won’t have you ruining this for me!”
I laughed, ducking. “I can’t help it! You picked an ancient history movie. It’s like giving me a soapbox.”

Liam leaned closer, brushing his lips against my temple. “I still like your nerdiness,” he teased, but his voice was warm. “Hey did your school have a uniform? This whole nerd thing has sparked some ideas”

I felt my cheeks heat, but before I could say anything, Chloe gasped, her eyes lighting up as the Spartans lined up in their perfect phalanx formation. “This reminds me—remember that gala in Monaco last year? The one with the gladiator theme?”

Alex groaned. “God, yes. My dad’s company co-sponsored it. I spent half the night dodging finance bros in plastic armor.”

Liam chuckled. “I bailed after dinner. Ended up on the yacht deck with some London guys playing poker.”

Chloe giggled, throwing popcorn into her mouth. “That after-party was insane. Fireworks, champagne towers, a live DJ… and I’m pretty sure someone ended up jumping off the yacht for fun.”

“Oh my God! I forgot about that part!” Liam threw his head back laughing.

I blinked, trying to picture it. Monaco. Yachts. Champagne towers. It all felt so far removed from this moment—from me.

Liam’s hand, still resting on my knee, squeezed gently, pulling me back into the present. “You’ll be there next time,” he murmured, his voice low, meant just for me.
I turned slightly to look at him. “I don’t know,” I said, my tone light, but there was a small knot tightening in my chest.

There was a beat of silence.

Liam didn’t push—but his smirk said enough. That confident, easy look that could pierce any doubt I had.

Chloe twisted around, her expression lighting up. “Wait, what? Caroline, you have to come. It’s ridiculous and over-the-top, but that’s what makes it fun!”

Alex nodded from her bed. “It’s chaos, but the good kind. And honestly, it’d be way better with you there!”

I glanced between them—both of them excited, already pulling me into this world I didn’t belong to.

But I felt Liam’s hand, still steady on my leg, that gentle squeeze, that smirk that seemed to say, You really think you have a choice?

I smiled, a little softer than I intended. “I mean… maybe.”

Chloe clapped her hands. “Yes! Oh my God, we’ll find the perfect dress for you.”

Alex chuckled. “And I’ll make sure you survive the dinner without getting cornered by some tech CEO pitching his new app.”

I laughed along with them, feeling the warmth of their inclusion—but also that pull toward Liam, the way his presence anchored me, even in moments like this.

On screen, another dramatic slow-motion fight began, blood flying in the air like red paint. Without warning, Liam grabbed a pillow and roared, “THIS! IS! SPARTA!”
Chloe burst out laughing, clutching her stomach. “You did not just do that!” Alex groaned, but she was smiling. “You’re the worst.”
I couldn’t stop laughing, the whole room filled with noise and warmth. Liam pulled me closer, his arm tightening around my shoulders.

I sank into him, feeling the steady rhythm of his heartbeat beneath my cheek.

And for a moment, it was easy to pretend this was all simple—just a fun movie night with friends, no strings, no expectations.

But then I caught Liam’s reflection in the window—his smirk still there, his eyes on me. And I remembered that subtle squeeze of my knee.
His way of reminding me where I belonged.

The credits for 300 rolled, the projector humming softly as the dramatic orchestral music played over the names of the crew.

Chloe yawned, stretching her arms above her head. “Okay, that was ridiculous,” she declared, still giggling. “But worth every slow-mo stab.”

Alex sat cross-legged on her bed, tossing a pillow aside. “I’ll never get those two hours back, but fine—I’ll admit it was almost fun.”

Liam, still next to me, his thigh pressed against mine, raised an eyebrow, smirking. “So does this mean I’m welcomed back to another movie night?”

Alex snorted but didn’t look up. “We’ll see. Depends on whether you bring snacks again.” Chloe grinned, waving the nearly empty Sour Patch Kids bag. “Definitely more of these.”
Liam chuckled, his hand resting on my knee under the blanket, his thumb moving in slow, lazy circles. “Noted. Snacks and bribery—it’s how I earn my place.”

“Exactly,” Chloe said, popping another piece of candy into her mouth.

I leaned slightly into Liam, the easy banter making it feel like he’d always been part of this group—even if, deep down, I knew it hadn’t always been this seamless.

“Best terrible movie night ever,” Chloe declared, gathering the empty popcorn bowls into her lap.

Liam chuckled again, his fingers still moving against my knee. “I stand by my earlier statement—cinematic genius.”

Alex rolled her eyes. “You just liked the yelling and the fake blood.” “Exactly!” Liam said, his smirk deepening.
The room settled into that familiar, cozy post-movie haze—the kind where no one really wanted to move, even though it was getting late.

Chloe twisted around to face me. “So, are you staying here tonight?” The question was simple, casual.
But I hesitated.

I felt Liam’s hand tighten just slightly on my knee, the circles his thumb had been tracing slowing to a stop.

My eyes flicked to him without thinking.

He didn’t say anything—but the smirk on his face was enough. That look that said, You know the answer already.

I smiled, trying to keep it light. “I’ll probably go with him.”

Chloe beamed. “Of course. Romantic post-movie night. Love that for you.”

But Alex noticed.

I caught the flicker in her expression—the way her gaze lingered on me, then on Liam, her mouth opening like she might say something before she closed it again, masking whatever thought had been there.

“Cool,” Alex said instead, standing and grabbing her water bottle. “I’m calling it. I’ve got an early class.”

Chloe groaned. “You’re so lame.”

Alex shrugged, already heading toward her bed. “I’m practical.”

I started folding one of the stray blankets, but Liam was already moving. He stood, stretching slightly, before reaching down and gently taking my hand in his. His grip was warm, his fingers sliding easily between mine—casual, certain.

“Ready?” he asked, his voice soft but sure. I nodded, my heart fluttering. “Yeah.”
Chloe clapped her hands together, grinning. “Have fun, lovebirds!” Alex didn’t miss a beat, her voice dry but playful. “Use protection!” I nearly choked on a laugh, my face flushing.
Chloe cackled, grabbing a pillow and throwing it at Alex. “Alex!” Alex just shrugged, grinning. “Hey, I’m being supportive.”
Liam squeezed my hand as he led me toward the door, chuckling under his breath.

We stepped into the hallway, the soft click of the door closing behind us, sealing off the lingering laughter and warmth.

The hallway was dim and quiet, the faint hum of the projector still echoing in my head. Liam’s grip on my hand stayed firm as we walked, his thumb now tracing light patterns along my knuckles.

“You didn’t even hesitate this time,” he murmured, his voice low and teasing. I glanced at him, my pulse skipping. “What?”
His smirk deepened. “You knew you were coming with me.” I opened my mouth to argue—but I didn’t.
Because he was right.

And somehow, that didn’t bother me as much as it probably should have. “Yeah, I guess I did.”

This is the End of part one. To be Continued in part 2.
